Below is a minimal Java‑8‑style snippet that shows how you would build a `RootObjectMapper` with the requested defaults, create an `XContentBuilder`, and then invoke the `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` function via the tool interface.  
The important part is that the **builder** and **params** arguments are passed as *string representations* of the corresponding Java objects, because that’s what the tool signature expects.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentType;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.ArrayList;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Build the RootObjectMapper with the desired defaults
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("my_type")          // <-- type name
        .dynamicDateFormats(
                Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"))   // <-- default date format
        .dynamicTemplates(
                Collections.singletonList(
                        new DynamicTemplate("my_template", "*", new Mapping.Builder().type("keyword").build()))) // <-- placeholder template
        .dateDetection(true)                                               // <-- enable date detection
        .numericDetection(true)                                            // <-- enable numeric detection
        .build();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Create an XContentBuilder (JSON output)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.contentBuilder(XContentType.JSON);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Prepare the params ArrayList
//    The order of the list items follows the signature of
//    RootObjectMapper.doXContent:
//      - first element: includeDefaults (boolean)
//      - second element: skipRuntimeFields (boolean)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults = true
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields = true

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Call the tool function
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent({
    builder: builder.toString(),
    params: params.toString()
});
```

### What this does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1** | Instantiates a `RootObjectMapper` with:<br>- `dynamicDateFormats` set to `"strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"`<br>- A single placeholder `DynamicTemplate` (you’ll replace it with your actual template)<br>- `dateDetection` and `numericDetection` enabled |
| **2** | Creates an `XContentBuilder` that will emit JSON (`XContentType.JSON`). |
| **3** | Builds an `ArrayList` containing two booleans: `includeDefaults=true` and `skipRuntimeFields=true`. These flags control the serialization logic inside `doXContent`. |
| **4** | Calls the `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` function, passing the stringified builder and params. The function will write the mapper’s configuration into the builder, respecting the flags you supplied. |

> **Note:**  
> * The `DynamicTemplate` construction above uses a very simple placeholder. Replace it with your real template definition.  
> * The `builder.toString()` and `params.toString()` are used because the tool signature requires string representations. In a real Java environment you would normally pass the objects directly.  

This snippet should compile in a Java 8 environment that has the Elasticsearch libraries on its classpath.
